// 2022 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2022\2022.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2022, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"\"Rapid Financing Instrument\" and \"Rapid Credit Facility\" are related to the provisions of lending by which one of the following?","A":"Asian Development Bank","B":"International Monetary Fund","C":"United Nations Environment Programme Finance Initiative","D":"World Bank","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["IMF","Rapid Financing Instrument","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Rapid Financing Instrument (RFI) and Rapid Credit Facility (RCF) are lending facilities of the International Monetary Fund (IMF). They are designed to provide quick financial assistance to member countries facing urgent balance of payments needs. RFI is generally available to a broad range of IMF members, while RCF provides concessional assistance to eligible low-income countries.","revisionPoints":"RFI → IMF facility\nRCF → IMF facility\nPurpose → Rapid financial assistance\nRFI → Balance of payments needs\nRCF → Low-income countries"},
{"no":2,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. An increase in Nominal Effective Exchange Rate (NEER) indicates the appreciation of rupee.\n2. An increase in the Real Effective Exchange Rate (REER) indicates an improvement in trade competitiveness.\n3. An increasing trend in domestic inflation relative to inflation in other countries is likely to cause an increasing divergence between NEER and REER.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["NEER","REER","Exchange Rate","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: An increase in NEER means the rupee has appreciated against the currencies in the trade-weighted basket.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: A higher REER generally indicates that the rupee has appreciated in real terms, which can reduce trade competitiveness.\nStatement 3 is correct: If domestic inflation rises faster than inflation in other countries, the REER can rise relative to the NEER, creating a greater divergence between them.","revisionPoints":"NEER ↑ → Rupee appreciation\nREER ↑ → Competitiveness ↓\nNEER → Nominal exchange rate\nREER → Inflation-adjusted\nDomestic inflation ↑ → REER ↑"},
{"no":3,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. If the inflation is too high, Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is likely to buy government securities.\n2. If the rupee is rapidly depreciating, RBI is likely to sell dollars in the market.\n3. If interest rates in the USA or European Union were to fall, that is likely to induce RBI to buy dollars.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["RBI","Open Market Operations","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: When inflation is too high, RBI generally follows a contractionary monetary policy and may sell government securities to reduce money supply.\nStatement 2 is correct: When the rupee depreciates rapidly, RBI can sell dollars from its foreign exchange reserves to increase dollar supply and support the rupee.\nStatement 3 is correct: If interest rates in the USA or EU fall, capital may move towards India, and RBI may buy dollars to manage excessive foreign-currency inflows and prevent sharp rupee appreciation.","revisionPoints":"High inflation → Sell securities\nRupee depreciation → Sell dollars\nForeign rates ↓ → Dollar inflows ↑\nRBI dollar buying → Prevent appreciation\nRBI → Forex market intervention"},
{"no":4,"text":"With reference to the \"G20 Common Framework\", consider the following statements:\n1. It is an initiative endorsed by the G20 together with the Paris Club.\n2. It is an initiative to support Low Income Countries with unsustainable debt.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["G20","Common Framework","Debt Restructuring","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The G20 Common Framework for Debt Treatments was endorsed by the G20 countries and the Paris Club in 2020. It provides a coordinated approach for addressing unsustainable debt situations in low-income countries. It aims to bring different official creditors together for debt restructuring and treatment. Therefore, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Common Framework → G20 + Paris Club\nLaunched → 2020\nTarget → Low-income countries\nPurpose → Debt restructuring\nFocus → Unsustainable debt"},
{"no":5,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy, what are the advantages of \"Inflation-Indexed Bonds (IIBs)\"?\n1. Government can reduce the coupon rates on its borrowing by way of IIBs.\n2. IIBs provide protection to the investors from uncertainty regarding inflation.\n3. The interest received as well as capital gains in IIBs are not taxable.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Inflation-Indexed Bonds","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Inflation-Indexed Bonds (IIBs) protect investors against inflation, so the government may be able to borrow at a lower coupon rate than on conventional bonds.\nStatement 2 is correct: IIBs provide investors protection against the uncertainty of inflation, as the principal and interest are linked to inflation.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Interest income and capital gains from IIBs are not completely tax-free.","revisionPoints":"IIBs → Inflation protection\nPrincipal → Inflation-linked\nInvestor → Reduced inflation risk\nGovernment → Lower borrowing cost\nTax → Interest taxable"},
{"no":6,"text":"With reference to foreign-owned e-commerce firms operating in India, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. They can sell their own goods in addition to offering their platforms as market-places.\n2. The degree to which they can own big sellers on their platforms is limited.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["E-commerce","FDI Policy","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Under India's foreign direct investment (FDI) rules for e-commerce, foreign-owned e-commerce companies following the marketplace model cannot sell goods as an inventory they own. Therefore, Statement 1 is incorrect. Their ability to control or own major sellers on their platforms is also restricted to prevent the marketplace from effectively becoming an inventory-based retailer. Hence, Statement 2 is also incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Marketplace Model → Platform for sellers\nInventory Model → Own goods\nForeign FDI → Marketplace permitted\nOwn Inventory → Not permitted\nSeller Control → Restricted"},
{"no":7,"text":"Which of the following activities constitute real sector in the economy?\n1. Farmers harvesting their crops\n2. Textile mills converting raw cotton into fabrics\n3. A commercial bank lending money to a trading company\n4. A corporate body issuing Rupee Denominated Bonds overseas\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Real Sector","National Income","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The real sector includes activities that involve the production of goods and services. Farmers harvesting crops and textile mills converting cotton into fabric are direct production activities, so Statements 1 and 2 belong to the real sector. Bank lending and issuing bonds are financial-sector activities, so Statements 3 and 4 do not belong to the real sector.","revisionPoints":"Real Sector → Goods + Services\nFarming → Real sector\nTextile production → Real sector\nBank lending → Financial sector\nBond issuance → Financial sector"},
{"no":8,"text":"Which one of the following situations best reflects \"Indirect Transfers\" often talked about in media recently with reference to India?","A":"An Indian company investing in a foreign enterprise and paying taxes to the foreign country on the profits arising out of its investment.","B":"A foreign company investing in India and paying taxes to the country of its base on the profits arising out of its investment.","C":"An Indian company purchases tangible assets in a foreign country and sells such assets after their value increases and transfers the proceeds to India.","D":"A foreign company transfers shares and such shares derive their substantial value from assets located in India.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Indirect Transfer","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"An indirect transfer occurs when shares of a foreign company are transferred outside India, but those shares derive their substantial value from assets located in India. Although the transaction takes place outside India, it can have a connection with Indian assets for taxation purposes. Therefore, Option D correctly describes an indirect transfer.","revisionPoints":"Indirect Transfer → Foreign shares\nUnderlying Value → Indian assets\nTransaction → Outside India\nTax Link → Indian assets\nPurpose → Prevent tax avoidance"},
{"no":9,"text":"With reference to the expenditure made by an organisation or a company, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. Acquiring new technology is capital expenditure.\n2. Debt financing is considered capital expenditure, while equity financing is considered revenue expenditure.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Capital Expenditure","Revenue Expenditure","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Acquiring new technology creates a long-term asset or improves the productive capacity of an organisation, so it is generally treated as capital expenditure.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Debt financing and equity financing are sources of finance, not types of expenditure. Therefore, they cannot be classified as capital or revenue expenditure.","revisionPoints":"Capital Expenditure → Long-term assets\nNew Technology → Capital expenditure\nRevenue Expenditure → Routine expenses\nDebt Financing → Source of funds\nEquity Financing → Source of funds"},
{"no":10,"text":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. A share of the household financial savings goes towards government borrowings.\n2. Dated securities issued at market-related rates in auctions form a large component of internal debt.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Internal Debt","Household Savings","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Both statements are correct. A part of household financial savings is invested in government securities through banks, insurance companies, provident funds, mutual funds, etc., helping finance government borrowing. Dated government securities issued through market-based auctions at market-related interest rates form a major component of the government's internal debt. Therefore, both Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Household Savings → Government borrowing\nGovernment Securities → Public borrowing\nDated Securities → Major internal debt\nAuction → Market-related rates\nInternal Debt → Domestic sources"},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Pursuant to the report of H. N. Sanyal Committee, the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 was passed.\n2. The Constitution of India empowers the Supreme Court and the High Courts to punish for contempt of themselves.\n3. The Constitution of India defines Civil Contempt and Criminal Contempt.\n4. In India, the Parliament is vested with the powers to make laws on Contempt of Court.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 4","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Contempt of Court","Sanyal Committee","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. The H. N. Sanyal Committee examined the law relating to contempt of courts, which led to the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971. The Constitution gives the Supreme Court (Article 129) and High Courts (Article 215) the power to punish for contempt of themselves.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because the Constitution does not define civil and criminal contempt; these are defined under the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971. Parliament has the power to make laws on contempt of court.","revisionPoints":"H. N. Sanyal Committee → Contempt law\nContempt of Courts Act → 1971\nSupreme Court → Article 129\nHigh Courts → Article 215\nCivil/Criminal Contempt → Defined by 1971 Act"},
{"no":12,"text":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\n1. Government law officers and legal firms are recognised as advocates, but corporate lawyers and patent attorneys are excluded from recognition as advocates.\n2. Bar Councils have the power to lay down the rules relating to legal education and recognition of law colleges.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Bar Council","Advocates Act","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Under the Advocates Act, 1961, an advocate is a person enrolled on the roll of a State Bar Council. The law does not recognise categories such as government law officers and legal firms as separate classes of “advocates” while excluding corporate lawyers and patent attorneys in the manner stated.\nStatement 2 is correct: The Bar Council of India (BCI) has the power to lay down standards of legal education and recognise universities whose law degrees qualify for enrolment as advocates.","revisionPoints":"Advocates Act → 1961\nBar Council of India → Legal education standards\nBCI → Recognises law degrees\nState Bar Councils → Advocate enrolment\nLegal Education → BCI regulation"},
{"no":13,"text":"\"Invasive Species Specialist Group\" that develops Global Invasive Species Database belongs to which one of the following organizations?","A":"The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN)","B":"The United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP)","C":"The United Nations World Commission for Environment and Development","D":"The World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF)","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["IUCN","Invasive Species","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) is a specialist group under the Species Survival Commission (SSC) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN). It manages the Global Invasive Species Database (GISD), which provides information on invasive alien species and their impacts on biodiversity. Therefore, the correct answer is IUCN, not UNEP.","revisionPoints":"ISSG → IUCN\nISSG → Species Survival Commission\nGISD → Invasive species database\nIUCN → Red List\nInvasive Species → Biodiversity threat"},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following fauna:\n1. Lion-tailed Macaque\n2. Malabar Civet\n3. Sambar Deer\nHow many of the above are generally nocturnal or most active after sunset?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Nocturnal Animals","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Malabar Civet and Sambar Deer are generally nocturnal or most active after sunset, so Statements 2 and 3 are correct. The Lion-tailed Macaque is mainly diurnal, meaning it is active during the daytime. Therefore, only two of the three animals are generally nocturnal.","revisionPoints":"Lion-tailed Macaque → Diurnal\nMalabar Civet → Nocturnal\nSambar Deer → Nocturnal\nNocturnal → Active at night\nDiurnal → Active during day"},
{"no":15,"text":"Which of the following organisms perform waggle dance for others of their kin to indicate the direction and the distance to a source of their food?","A":"Butterflies","B":"Dragonflies","C":"Honeybees","D":"Wasps","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Honeybees","Waggle Dance","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The waggle dance is a special communication behaviour performed by honeybees, not dragonflies. A worker honeybee performs the dance to communicate the direction and approximate distance of a food source to other bees in the colony. Therefore, the given answer (B) Dragonflies is incorrect; the correct answer is (C) Honeybees.","revisionPoints":"Waggle Dance → Honeybees\nPurpose → Food location\nDirection → Sun’s position\nDistance → Dance duration\nCommunication → Colony members"},
{"no":16,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Some mushrooms have medicinal properties.\n2. Some mushrooms have psychoactive properties.\n3. Some mushrooms have insecticidal properties.\n4. Some mushrooms have bioluminescent properties.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Mushrooms","Fungi","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct. Some mushrooms have medicinal properties, some contain compounds with psychoactive effects, and some species are bioluminescent and produce light. Statement 3 is generally considered incorrect in this context because mushrooms are not commonly classified as having insecticidal properties. Therefore, three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Mushrooms → Medicinal properties\nSome species → Psychoactive\nSome species → Bioluminescent\nBioluminescence → Light production\nMushrooms → Fungi kingdom"},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Indian squirrels:\n1. They build nests by making burrows in the ground.\n2. They store their food materials like nuts and seeds in the ground.\n3. They are omnivorous.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Indian Squirrels","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All three statements are incorrect. Indian squirrels generally build their nests above the ground, often in trees, rather than making burrows. They do not generally store nuts and seeds by burying them in the ground like some temperate-zone squirrels. They are mainly herbivorous, feeding on fruits, seeds, nuts and other plant material, though some may occasionally eat insects.","revisionPoints":"Indian Squirrels → Tree-dwelling\nNests → Above ground\nFood storage → Not underground\nDiet → Mainly herbivorous\nHabitat → Trees + vegetation"},
{"no":18,"text":"With reference to the writs issued by the Courts in India, consider the following statements:\n1. Mandamus will not lie against a private organisation unless it is entrusted with a public duty.\n2. Mandamus will not lie against a Company even though it may be a Government Company.\n3. Any public minded person can be a petitioner to move the Court to obtain the writ of Quo Warranto.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Writs","Mandamus","Quo Warranto","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All three statements are correct. Mandamus can be issued against a private organisation when it is performing a public duty, but it generally does not lie against a purely private body. Even a Government Company is not automatically subject to mandamus merely because the government owns it; the nature of the duty is important. Quo Warranto can be sought by any public-minded person to question the legality of a person's occupation of a public office. Therefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Mandamus → Public duty\nPrivate body → Public function required\nGovernment Company → Not automatically Mandamus\nQuo Warranto → Public office\nPetitioner → Any public-minded person"},
{"no":19,"text":"With reference to Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission, consider the following statements:\n1. Private and public hospitals must adopt it.\n2. As it aims to achieve universal health coverage, every citizen of India should be part of it ultimately.\n3. It has seamless portability across the country.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Participation of private and public healthcare facilities in the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM) is not mandatory; they participate through the digital ecosystem.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: ABDM aims to create a digital health infrastructure, but it does not mean that every citizen is legally required to register.\nStatement 3 is correct: ABDM enables seamless portability of digital health records and services across the country, supporting continuity of healthcare.","revisionPoints":"ABDM → Digital health ecosystem\nParticipation → Voluntary\nCitizen registration → Not mandatory\nHealth records → Digital\nPortability → Across India"},
{"no":20,"text":"With reference to Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha, consider the following statements:\n1. As per the Rules of Procedure and Conduct of Business in Lok Sabha, the election of Deputy Speaker shall be held on such date as the Speaker may fix.\n2. There is a mandatory provision that the election of a candidate as Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha shall be from either the principal opposition party or the ruling party.\n3. The Deputy Speaker has the same power as the Speaker when presiding over the sitting of the House and no appeal lies against his rulings.\n4. The well-established parliamentary practice regarding the appointment of Deputy Speaker is that the motion is moved by the Speaker and duly seconded by the Prime Minister.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Deputy Speaker","Lok Sabha","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. Under the Lok Sabha Rules, the Speaker fixes the date for the election of the Deputy Speaker.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because there is no mandatory constitutional or legal provision that the Deputy Speaker must belong to either the ruling party or the principal opposition party.\nStatement 3 is correct because when presiding over the House, the Deputy Speaker has the same powers as the Speaker, and his rulings cannot be appealed against.\nStatement 4 is incorrect because there is no such established rule that the Speaker must move the motion and the Prime Minister must second it. Hence, A. 1 and 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Deputy Speaker → Article 93\nElection date → Speaker fixes\nParty requirement → No mandatory rule\nPresiding powers → Same as Speaker\nRulings → No appeal"},
{"no":21,"text":"Among the following crops, which one is the most important anthropogenic source of both methane and nitrous oxide?","A":"Cotton","B":"Rice","C":"Sugarcane","D":"Wheat","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Methane","Rice Cultivation","Greenhouse Gases","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Rice cultivation is an important anthropogenic source of both methane (CH₄) and nitrous oxide (N₂O). Flooded rice fields create oxygen-poor conditions where microorganisms produce methane. The use of nitrogen fertilizers in rice cultivation can also release nitrous oxide. Therefore, among the given crops, Rice is the correct answer – B.","revisionPoints":"Rice → Methane\nFlooded fields → CH₄\nNitrogen fertilizer → N₂O\nAnaerobic conditions → Methane\nRice farming → GHG source"},
{"no":22,"text":"\"System of Rice Intensification\" of cultivation, in which alternate wetting and drying of rice fields is practised, results in:\n1. Reduced seed requirement\n2. Reduced methane production\n3. Reduced electricity consumption\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["System of Rice Intensification","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The System of Rice Intensification (SRI) uses fewer seeds and practices alternate wetting and drying (AWD) instead of continuous flooding. This reduces water requirement and therefore electricity used for irrigation. Since fields are not continuously flooded, anaerobic conditions decrease, leading to lower methane production. Hence, all three statements are correct – D. 1, 2 and 3.","revisionPoints":"SRI → Fewer seeds\nAWD → Less water\nLess flooding → Less CH₄\nLess irrigation → Less electricity\nSRI → Sustainable farming"},
{"no":23,"text":"Which one of the following lakes of West Africa has become dry and turned into a desert?","A":"Lake Victoria","B":"Lake Faguibine","C":"Lake Oguta","D":"Lake Volta","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["West Africa","Lakes","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Lake Faguibine is located in Mali, West Africa, and is part of the Niger River’s inland delta system. Due to recurrent droughts, reduced rainfall, and changes in the Niger River’s water flow, the lake has largely dried up, with parts becoming desert-like.","revisionPoints":"Lake Faguibine → Mali\nRegion → West Africa\nRiver system → Niger\nMajor issue → Drying\nCauses → Drought + rainfall decline"},
{"no":24,"text":"Gandikota canyon of South India was created by which one of the following rivers?","A":"Cauvery","B":"Manjira","C":"Pennar","D":"Tungabhadra","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Pennar River","Canyons","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Gandikota Canyon is located in Andhra Pradesh and was formed by the Pennar (Penna) River cutting through the Gandikota hills over a long period of time. The deep gorge and steep rock walls were created mainly through river erosion.","revisionPoints":"Gandikota → Andhra Pradesh\nRiver → Pennar\nLandform → Canyon\nFormation → River erosion\nPennar → Peninsular river"},
{"no":25,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nPeak\nMountains\n1. Namcha Barwa\nGarhwal Himalaya\n2. Nanda Devi\nKumaon Himalaya\n3. Nokrek\nSikkim Himalaya\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Himalayas","Peaks","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Namcha Barwa is located in the Eastern Himalayas, in Arunachal Pradesh, not the Garhwal Himalayas.\nPair 2 is correct: Nanda Devi is a major peak of the Kumaon Himalayas in Uttarakhand.\nPair 3 is incorrect: Nokrek is located in the Garo Hills of Meghalaya, not the Sikkim Himalayas.","revisionPoints":"Namcha Barwa → Eastern Himalaya\nNanda Devi → Kumaon Himalaya\nNokrek → Garo Hills\nNanda Devi → Uttarakhand\nNokrek → Meghalaya"},
{"no":26,"text":"The term \"Levant\", often heard in the news, roughly corresponds to which of the following regions?","A":"Region along the eastern Mediterranean shores","B":"Region along North African shores stretching from Egypt to Morocco","C":"Region along Persian Gulf and Horn of Africa","D":"The entire coastal areas of Mediterranean Sea","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Levant","Middle East Geography","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Levant is a historical-geographical term referring roughly to the eastern Mediterranean region. It generally includes countries such as Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Palestine and Jordan, though its exact boundaries vary depending on the context. Therefore, the correct answer is A. Region along the eastern Mediterranean shores.","revisionPoints":"Levant → Eastern Mediterranean\nRegion → West Asia\nIncludes → Syria, Lebanon\nMediterranean → Eastern shores\nBoundaries → Not fixed"},
{"no":27,"text":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Azerbaijan\n2. Kyrgyzstan\n3. Tajikistan\n4. Turkmenistan\n5. Uzbekistan\nWhich of the above have borders with Afghanistan?","A":"1, 2 and 5 only","B":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only","C":"3, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Afghanistan","Central Asia","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Afghanistan shares borders with Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan among the countries given. Tajikistan borders Afghanistan in the northeast, while Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan border it in the north. Azerbaijan and Kyrgyzstan do not share a border with Afghanistan.","revisionPoints":"Afghanistan → Central Asia\nTajikistan → Borders Afghanistan\nTurkmenistan → Borders Afghanistan\nUzbekistan → Borders Afghanistan\nAzerbaijan, Kyrgyzstan → No border"},
{"no":28,"text":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\n1. Monazite is a source of rare earths.\n2. Monazite contains thorium.\n3. Monazite occurs naturally in the entire Indian coastal sands.\n4. In India, Government bodies only can process or export monazite.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 2 and 4 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Monazite","Rare Earths","Thorium","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Monazite is an important source of rare earth elements such as cerium and lanthanum.\nStatement 2 is correct because monazite contains thorium and can also contain uranium.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because monazite is found only in certain coastal sands, not along the entire Indian coast.\nStatement 4 is correct because monazite is a prescribed substance and its processing/export is subject to government regulation. Hence, B. 1, 2 and 4 only.","revisionPoints":"Monazite → Rare earth source\nMonazite → Thorium-bearing\nFound → Coastal sands\nNot → Entire coast\nMonazite → Government regulated"},
{"no":29,"text":"In the northern hemisphere, the longest day of the year normally occurs in the:","A":"First half of the month of June","B":"Second half of the month of June","C":"First half of the month of July","D":"Second half of the month of July","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Solstice","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"In the Northern Hemisphere, the longest day of the year occurs around 21 June, known as the Summer Solstice. On this day, the Sun is directly overhead at the Tropic of Cancer (23½° N), giving the Northern Hemisphere its maximum daylight. Therefore, the correct answer is B. Second half of June.","revisionPoints":"Longest day → 21 June\nEvent → Summer Solstice\nSun overhead → Tropic of Cancer\nNorthern Hemisphere → Longest day\nSouthern Hemisphere → Shortest day"},
{"no":30,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nWetland/Lake\nLocation\n1. Hokera Wetland\nPunjab\n2. Renuka Wetland\nHimachal Pradesh\n3. Rudrasagar Lake\nTripura\n4. Sasthamkotta Lake\nTamil Nadu\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Wetlands","Ramsar Sites","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Hokera Wetland is located in Jammu and Kashmir, not Punjab.\nPair 2 is correct: Renuka Wetland is in Himachal Pradesh.\nPair 3 is correct: Rudrasagar Lake is in Tripura.\nPair 4 is incorrect: Sasthamkotta Lake is in Kerala, not Tamil Nadu.","revisionPoints":"Hokera → Jammu & Kashmir\nRenuka → Himachal Pradesh\nRudrasagar → Tripura\nSasthamkotta → Kerala\nCorrect pairs → 2 and 3"},
{"no":31,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Aarogya Setu\n2. CoWIN\n3. DigiLocker\n4. DIKSHA\nWhich of the above are built on top of open-source digital platforms?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Open Source","Digital Platforms","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All four platforms—Aarogya Setu, CoWIN, DigiLocker and DIKSHA—were developed using open-source digital technologies/platforms. Open-source systems allow the underlying source code or components to be accessed, reviewed and reused, supporting transparency and wider development. Hence, all four are correct.","revisionPoints":"Aarogya Setu → Open-source\nCoWIN → Open-source\nDigiLocker → Open-source\nDIKSHA → Open-source\nAnswer → All four"},
{"no":32,"text":"With reference to Web 3.0, consider the following statements:\n1. Web 3.0 technology enables people to control their own data.\n2. In Web 3.0 world, there can be blockchain-based social networks.\n3. Web 3.0 is operated by users collectively rather than a corporation.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Web 3.0","Blockchain","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Web 3.0 refers to a more decentralized and user-centric version of the internet. It aims to give users greater control and ownership of their data. It can use blockchain technology to create decentralized social networks and other applications. Unlike Web 2.0, where large corporations often control platforms and data, Web 3.0 emphasizes collective/user participation and decentralized governance. Hence, all three statements are correct — D. 1, 2 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Web 3.0 → Decentralized web\nData → User-controlled\nBlockchain → Key technology\nSocial networks → Decentralized\nGovernance → User participation"},
{"no":33,"text":"With reference to \"Software as a Service (SaaS)\", consider the following statements:\n1. SaaS buyers can customise the user interface and can change data fields.\n2. SaaS users can access their data through their mobile devices.\n3. Outlook, Hotmail and Yahoo! Mail are forms of SaaS.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["SaaS","Cloud Computing","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Software as a Service (SaaS) is a model where software is provided over the internet, so users do not need to install and maintain it on their own computers. SaaS applications can allow users to customise interfaces and data fields and access their data through mobile devices. Services such as Outlook, Hotmail and Yahoo! Mail are examples of cloud-based SaaS services. Hence, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"SaaS → Software via Internet\nInterface → Customisable\nAccess → Mobile devices\nData → Cloud-based\nExamples → Outlook, Hotmail, Yahoo Mail"},
{"no":34,"text":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the idea behind the \"Fractional Orbital Bombardment System\" often talked about in media?","A":"A hypersonic missile is launched into space to counter the asteroid approaching the Earth and explode it in space.","B":"A spacecraft lands on another planet after making several orbital motions.","C":"A missile is put into a stable orbit around the Earth and deorbits over a target on the Earth.","D":"A spacecraft moves along a comet with the same speed and places a probe on its surface.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["FOBS","Missile Technology","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Fractional Orbital Bombardment System (FOBS) is a concept in which a missile carrying a warhead is placed into a partial Earth orbit and later deorbited toward a target. Unlike a conventional ballistic missile, it can approach the target from an unexpected direction, potentially making traditional missile-warning systems more difficult to use.","revisionPoints":"FOBS → Partial orbital path\nWarhead → Earth orbit\nDeorbit → Target\nApproach → Unexpected direction\nPurpose → Strategic delivery"},
{"no":35,"text":"Which one of the following is the context in which the term \"qubit\" is mentioned?","A":"Cloud Services","B":"Quantum Computing","C":"Visible Light Communication Technologies","D":"Wireless Communication Technologies","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Qubit","Quantum Computing","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"A qubit (quantum bit) is the basic unit of information in quantum computing. Unlike a classical bit, which can be either 0 or 1, a qubit can exist in a superposition of 0 and 1 until it is measured. Qubits can also show quantum entanglement, which helps quantum computers perform certain computations differently from classical computers.","revisionPoints":"Qubit → Quantum bit\nUsed in → Quantum computing\nClassical bit → 0 or 1\nQubit → Superposition\nQubit → Entanglement"},
{"no":36,"text":"Consider the following communication technologies:\n1. Closed-circuit Television\n2. Radio Frequency Identification\n3. Wireless Local Area Network\nWhich of the above are considered Short-Range devices/technologies?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["RFID","WLAN","Short-Range Devices","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All three technologies can be considered short-range communication technologies. CCTV is used for communication and monitoring within a limited area, while RFID uses radio waves to identify and track objects over short distances. WLAN, such as Wi-Fi, provides wireless network connectivity within a limited local area like a home, office or campus.","revisionPoints":"CCTV → Limited area\nRFID → Short-range identification\nWLAN → Local wireless network\nWi-Fi → WLAN technology\nAll three → Short-range"},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Biofilms can form on medical implants within human tissues.\n2. Biofilms can form on food and food processing surfaces.\n3. Biofilms can exhibit antibiotic resistance.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Biofilms","Antibiotic Resistance","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"A biofilm is a community of microorganisms that attaches to a surface and produces a protective layer around itself. Biofilms can develop on medical implants such as catheters and prosthetic devices, as well as on food and food-processing surfaces. The protective matrix can make microorganisms more difficult to eliminate and can contribute to antibiotic resistance. Hence, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Biofilm → Microbial community\nForms on → Medical implants\nForms on → Food surfaces\nProtective layer → Matrix\nBiofilm → Antibiotic resistance"},
{"no":38,"text":"Consider the following statements in respect of probiotics:\n1. Probiotics are made of both bacteria and yeast.\n2. The organisms in probiotics are found in foods we ingest but they do not naturally occur in our gut.\n3. Probiotics help in the digestion of milk sugars.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Probiotics","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Probiotics contain live beneficial microorganisms, mainly bacteria and sometimes yeast.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because some probiotic organisms are similar to microorganisms that naturally occur in the human gut.\nStatement 3 is correct because certain probiotics, such as Lactobacillus, help break down lactose (milk sugar) and can improve digestion. Hence, the correct answer is C. 1 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Probiotics → Live microorganisms\nTypes → Bacteria + yeast\nGut → Beneficial microbes\nLactose → Milk sugar\nProbiotics → Digestive health"},
{"no":39,"text":"In the context of vaccines manufactured to prevent COVID-19 pandemic, consider the following statements:\n1. The Serum Institute of India produced COVID-19 vaccine named Covishield using mRNA platform.\n2. Sputnik V vaccine is manufactured using vector based platform.\n3. COVAXIN is an inactivated pathogen based vaccine.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["COVID-19 Vaccines","Covishield","Covaxin","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Covishield, manufactured by the Serum Institute of India, uses a viral vector platform, not an mRNA platform.\nStatement 2 is correct: Sputnik V is a viral vector-based vaccine, using two different adenoviruses.\nStatement 3 is correct: Covaxin is an inactivated whole-virus vaccine, which uses an inactivated form of the virus to stimulate an immune response. Hence, the correct answer is B. 2 and 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Covishield → Viral vector\nSputnik V → Viral vector\nCovaxin → Inactivated virus\nmRNA vaccine → Not Covishield\nCovaxin → Whole-virus vaccine"},
{"no":40,"text":"If a major solar storm (solar flare) reaches the Earth, which of the following are the possible effects on the Earth?\n1. GPS and navigation systems could fail.\n2. Tsunamis could occur at equatorial regions.\n3. Power grids could be damaged.\n4. Intense auroras could occur over much of the Earth.\n5. Forest fires could take place over much of the planet.\n6. Orbits of the satellites could be disturbed.\n7. Shortwave radio communication of the aircraft flying over polar regions could be interrupted.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1, 2, 4 and 5 only","B":"2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 only","C":"1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Solar Storm","Solar Flare","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"A major solar storm can disturb Earth's magnetosphere and ionosphere, causing GPS and navigation errors, power-grid damage, satellite-orbit disturbances, and intense auroras. It can also disrupt shortwave radio communication, especially for aircraft operating in polar regions. However, solar storms do not directly cause tsunamis or widespread forest fires. Hence, 1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 are correct — C.","revisionPoints":"Solar storm → Geomagnetic disturbance\nGPS → Can be disrupted\nPower grids → Can be damaged\nAuroras → Become intense\nSatellites → Orbits disturbed\nPolar radio → Communication affected\nTsunamis/forest fires → Not direct effects"},
{"no":41,"text":"\"Climate Action Tracker\" which monitors the emission reduction pledges of different countries is a:","A":"Database created by coalition of research organisations","B":"Wing of \"Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\"","C":"Committee under \"United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change\"","D":"Agency promoted and financed by United Nations Environment Programme and World Bank","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Climate Action Tracker","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Climate Action Tracker (CAT) is an independent scientific analysis platform that tracks and assesses the climate policies and emission-reduction commitments of countries. It is a collaboration of research organisations, not a body under the IPCC or UNFCCC. It evaluates whether countries' climate actions are consistent with the goals of the Paris Agreement. Hence, the correct answer is A. Database created by coalition of research organisations.","revisionPoints":"CAT → Climate Action Tracker\nNature → Independent platform\nRun by → Research organisations\nTracks → Emission pledges\nAssesses → Climate policies"},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. \"The Climate Group\" is an international non-profit organization that drives climate action by building large networks and runs them.\n2. The International Energy Agency in partnership with The Climate Group launched a global initiative \"EP100\".\n3. EP100 brings together leading companies committed to driving innovation in energy efficiency and increasing competitiveness while delivering on emission reduction goals.\n4. Some Indian companies are members of EP100.\n5. The International Energy Agency is the Secretariat to the \"Under2 Coalition\".\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2, 4 and 5","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2, 3 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["EP100","Under2 Coalition","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Climate Group is an international non-profit organisation that works with governments, businesses and other organisations to promote climate action.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: EP100 was launched by The Climate Group, in collaboration with the Global Alliance for Energy Productivity, not the International Energy Agency.\nStatement 3 is correct: EP100 brings together companies committed to improving energy efficiency and reducing emissions.\nStatement 4 is correct: Some Indian companies are members of EP100.\nStatement 5 is incorrect: The Climate Group provides the Secretariat for the Under2 Coalition, not the IEA. Therefore, the correct answer is B. 1, 3 and 4 only.","revisionPoints":"Climate Group → Non-profit\nEP100 → Energy efficiency\nEP100 → Corporate initiative\nIndian companies → EP100 members\nUnder2 Coalition → Climate Group Secretariat"},
{"no":43,"text":"\"If rainforests and tropical forests are the lungs of the Earth, then surely wetlands function as its kidneys.\" Which one of the following functions of wetlands best reflects the above statement?","A":"The water cycle in wetlands involves surface runoff, subsoil percolation and evaporation.","B":"Algae form the nutrient base upon which fish, crustaceans, molluscs, birds, reptiles and mammals thrive.","C":"Wetlands play a vital role in maintaining sedimentation balance and soil stabilization.","D":"Aquatic plants absorb heavy metals and excess nutrients.","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Wetlands","Ecosystem Services","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Wetlands are called the “kidneys of the Earth” because they help filter and purify water. Aquatic plants in wetlands absorb excess nutrients, pollutants and heavy metals from water, thereby improving water quality. This cleansing function is similar to how kidneys filter harmful substances from the blood. Hence, the correct answer is D. Aquatic plants absorb heavy metals and excess nutrients.","revisionPoints":"Wetlands → Natural filters\nAquatic plants → Absorb pollutants\nHeavy metals → Removed\nExcess nutrients → Absorbed\nWetlands → “Kidneys of Earth”"},
{"no":44,"text":"In the context of WHO Air Quality Guidelines, consider the following statements:\n1. The 24-hour mean of PM₂.₅ should not exceed 15 μg/m³ and annual mean of PM₂.₅ should not exceed 5 μg/m³.\n2. In a year, the highest levels of ozone pollution occur during the periods of inclement weather.\n3. PM₁₀ can penetrate the lung barrier and enter the bloodstream.\n4. Excessive ozone in the air can trigger asthma.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 3 and 4","B":"1 and 4 only","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1 and 2 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["WHO Air Quality Guidelines","PM2.5","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: WHO’s air-quality guideline values for PM₂.₅ are 5 μg/m³ for the annual mean and 15 μg/m³ for the 24-hour mean.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because ozone pollution is generally higher during sunny and hot conditions, not inclement weather.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because PM₁₀ is generally too large to penetrate deeply through the lung barrier into the bloodstream; finer particles such as PM₂.₅ pose greater concern.\nStatement 4 is correct because excessive ozone exposure can trigger or worsen asthma. Hence, the correct answer is B. 1 and 4 only.","revisionPoints":"PM₂.₅ annual → 5 μg/m³\nPM₂.₅ 24-hour → 15 μg/m³\nOzone → Sunny conditions\nPM₂.₅ → Finer particles\nOzone → Asthma trigger"},
{"no":45,"text":"With reference to \"Gucchi\" sometimes mentioned in the news, consider the following statements:\n1. It is a fungus.\n2. It grows in some Himalayan forest areas.\n3. It is commercially cultivated in the Himalayan foothills of north-eastern India.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Gucchi","Morel Mushroom","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Gucchi is a highly valued wild edible mushroom (fungus), also known as morel mushroom. It naturally grows in certain Himalayan forest areas, especially during the spring season. It is mainly wild-collected rather than commercially cultivated in the Himalayan foothills of north-eastern India. Therefore, Statements 1 and 2 are correct — C. 1 and 2.","revisionPoints":"Gucchi → Wild mushroom\nScientific group → Fungus\nHabitat → Himalayan forests\nCollection → Wild\nCultivation → Not commercial"},
{"no":46,"text":"With reference to polyethylene terephthalate (PET), the use of which is so widespread in our daily lives, consider the following statements:\n1. Its fibres can be blended with wool and cotton fibres to reinforce their properties.\n2. Containers made of it can be used to store alcoholic beverage.\n3. Bottles made of it can be recycled into other products.\n4. Articles made of it can be easily disposed of by incineration without causing greenhouse gas emissions.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 4","C":"1 and 4","D":"2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["PET","Plastic Recycling","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: PET fibres can be blended with wool and cotton to improve the strength and durability of fabrics.\nStatement 2 is incorrect in the context of this question because PET containers are not generally suitable for storing alcoholic beverages due to material and packaging limitations.\nStatement 3 is correct: PET bottles can be recycled into fibres, containers and other products.\nStatement 4 is incorrect because burning PET produces greenhouse gases, mainly carbon dioxide. Hence, the correct answer is A. 1 and 3","revisionPoints":"PET → Polyester\nFibres → Wool/cotton blend\nPET bottles → Recyclable\nIncineration → CO₂ emission\nPET → Common plastic"},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following is not a bird?","A":"Golden Mahseer","B":"Indian Nightjar","C":"Spoonbill","D":"White Ibis","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Birds","Golden Mahseer","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Golden Mahseer is not a bird; it is a freshwater fish found in rivers of the Indian subcontinent. The Indian Nightjar, Spoonbill, and White Ibis are all birds. Therefore, the correct answer is A. Golden Mahseer.","revisionPoints":"Golden Mahseer → Fish\nIndian Nightjar → Bird\nSpoonbill → Bird\nWhite Ibis → Bird\nMahseer → Freshwater fish"},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following are nitrogen-fixing plants?\n1. Alfalfa\n2. Amaranth\n3. Chickpea\n4. Clover\n5. Purslane (Kulfa)\n6. Spinach\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1, 3 and 4 only","B":"1, 3, 5 and 6 only","C":"2, 4, 5 and 6 only","D":"1, 2, 4, 5 and 6","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Nitrogen-fixing Plants","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Alfalfa, chickpea and clover are legumes belonging to the Fabaceae family and can fix atmospheric nitrogen through symbiotic relationships with Rhizobium bacteria in their root nodules. Amaranth, purslane (kulfa) and spinach are not nitrogen-fixing plants. Therefore, the correct answer is A. 1, 3 and 4 only.","revisionPoints":"Alfalfa → Nitrogen fixer\nChickpea → Nitrogen fixer\nClover → Nitrogen fixer\nRhizobium → Root nodules\nFabaceae → Legume family"},
{"no":49,"text":"\"Biorock technology\" is talked about in which one of the following situations?","A":"Restoration of damaged coral reefs","B":"Development of building materials using plant residues","C":"Identification of areas for exploration/extraction of shale gas","D":"Providing salt licks for wild animals in forests/protected areas","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biorock Technology","Coral Reefs","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Biorock technology is used for the restoration of damaged or degraded coral reefs. It uses a low-voltage electric current through underwater structures, causing minerals such as calcium carbonate to accumulate on them. This provides a suitable surface for coral growth and reef recovery. Hence, the correct answer is A. Restoration of damaged coral reefs.","revisionPoints":"Biorock → Coral restoration\nUsed → Damaged reefs\nElectric current → Low voltage\nMinerals → Calcium carbonate\nBenefit → Coral growth"},
{"no":50,"text":"The \"Miyawaki method\" is well known for the:","A":"Promotion of commercial farming in arid and semi-arid areas","B":"Development of gardens using genetically modified flora","C":"Creation of mini forests in urban areas","D":"Harvesting wind energy on coastal areas and on sea surfaces","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Miyawaki Method","Urban Forests","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Miyawaki method is a technique for creating dense, native mini-forests, especially in urban and degraded areas. It was developed by Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki. The method uses native plant species planted closely together, helping forests grow rapidly and improve biodiversity. Hence, the correct answer is C. Creation of mini forests in urban areas.","revisionPoints":"Miyawaki → Mini forests\nDeveloper → Akira Miyawaki\nPlants → Native species\nPlanting → High density\nBenefit → Biodiversity"},
{"no":51,"text":"In the Government of India Act, 1919, the functions of Provincial Government were divided into \"Reserved\" and \"Transferred\" subjects. Which of the following were treated as \"Reserved\" subjects?\n1. Administration of Justice\n2. Local Self-Government\n3. Land Revenue\n4. Police\nOptions:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Government of India Act 1919","Reserved Subjects","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1919 introduced Dyarchy in the provinces, dividing subjects into Reserved and Transferred categories. Reserved subjects were controlled by the Governor and his Executive Council and included Administration of Justice, Land Revenue and Police. Local Self-Government was a Transferred subject, handled by Indian ministers responsible to the legislative council. Hence, the correct answer is C. 1, 3 and 4.","revisionPoints":"GOI Act 1919 → Dyarchy\nReserved → Governor\nJustice → Reserved\nLand Revenue → Reserved\nPolice → Reserved\nLocal Self-Government → Transferred"},
{"no":52,"text":"In medieval India, the term \"Fanam\" referred to:","A":"Clothing","B":"Coins","C":"Ornaments","D":"Weapons","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Fanam","Coins","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Fanam was a small coin used in medieval South India, particularly in regions influenced by kingdoms such as Vijayanagara and later South Indian states. It was generally a small-denomination coin used in everyday trade and transactions. Hence, the correct answer is B. Coins.","revisionPoints":"Fanam → Coin\nType → Small denomination\nRegion → South India\nUse → Trade & transactions\nMedieval India → Currency"},
{"no":53,"text":"Consider the following freedom fighters:\n1. Barindra Kumar Ghosh\n2. Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee\n3. Rash Behari Bose\nWho of the above was/were actively associated with the Ghadar Party?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Ghadar Party","Freedom Fighters","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Rash Behari Bose was actively associated with the Ghadar movement and played an important role in the planned Ghadar uprising of 1915 in India. Barindra Kumar Ghosh was associated mainly with the revolutionary activities of Anushilan Samiti and Jugantar in Bengal. Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee was also associated with revolutionary activities in Bengal and later the Hindustan Republican Association, but not the Ghadar Party. Hence, the correct answer is D. 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Ghadar Party → 1913\nRash Behari Bose → Ghadar movement\nBarindra Ghosh → Jugantar\nJogesh Chandra → HRA\nGhadar → Revolutionary movement"},
{"no":54,"text":"With reference to the proposals of Cripps Mission, consider the following statements:\n1. The Constituent Assembly would have members nominated by the Provincial Assemblies as well as the Princely States.\n2. Any Province, which is not prepared to accept the new Constitution would have the right to sign a separate agreement with Britain regarding its future status.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Cripps Mission","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Under the Cripps Mission (1942) proposals, members of the Constituent Assembly were to be elected by the Provincial Legislative Assemblies, while representatives of the Princely States were to be nominated by their rulers. The statement incorrectly says members were nominated by both.\nStatement 2 is correct: Provinces that did not accept the new Constitution could opt out and enter into a separate agreement with Britain regarding their future status. Hence, the correct answer is B. 2 only.","revisionPoints":"Cripps Mission → 1942\nProvincial members → Elected\nPrincely States → Nominated\nOpt-out → Provinces\nSeparate agreement → Britain"},
{"no":55,"text":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following texts:\n1. Nettipakarana\n2. Parishishtaparvan\n3. Avadanasataka\n4. Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana\nWhich of the above are Jaina texts?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Jaina Texts","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Parishishtaparvan and Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana are important Jaina texts. Parishishtaparvan, written by Hemachandra, deals with Jain history and traditions. Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana is a major Jain work associated with Jinasena and Gunabhadra. Nettipakarana is a Buddhist text, while Avadanasataka is also a Buddhist Sanskrit text. Hence, the correct answer is B. 2 and 4 only.","revisionPoints":"Parishishtaparvan → Jain\nTrishashtilakshana Mahapurana → Jain\nNettipakarana → Buddhist\nAvadanasataka → Buddhist\nHemachandra → Jain scholar"},
{"no":56,"text":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following pairs:\nHistorical Person\nKnown as\n1. Aryadeva\nJaina scholar\n2. Dignaga\nBuddhist scholar\n3. Nathamuni\nVaishnava scholar\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"None of the pairs","B":"Only one pair","C":"Only two pairs","D":"All three pairs","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhist Scholars","Jaina Scholars","Vaishnava Scholars","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Aryadeva was a famous Buddhist scholar, associated with the Madhyamaka school and Nagarjuna, not a Jaina scholar.\nPair 2 is correct: Dignaga was a major Buddhist scholar known for his work on logic and epistemology.\nPair 3 is correct: Nathamuni was an important Vaishnava scholar and theologian, associated with the Sri Vaishnava tradition. Hence, 2 pairs are correctly matched — C. Only two pairs.","revisionPoints":"Aryadeva → Buddhist\nDignaga → Buddhist\nNathamuni → Vaishnava\nAryadeva → Madhyamaka\nDignaga → Buddhist logic"},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\n1. The first Mongol invasion of India happened during the reign of Jalal-ud-din Khalji.\n2. During the reign of Ala-ud-din Khalji, one Mongol assault marched up to Delhi and besieged the city.\n3. Muhammad-bin-Tughlaq temporarily lost portions of north-west of his kingdom to Mongols.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mongol Invasions","Khalji Dynasty","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The first major Mongol invasion of India occurred during the reign of Iltutmish, before Jalal-ud-din Khalji.\nStatement 2 is correct: During the reign of Ala-ud-din Khalji, a major Mongol army reached Delhi and besieged the city in 1303.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Muhammad-bin-Tughlaq did not temporarily lose north-western territories to the Mongols. Hence, the correct answer is B. 2 only.","revisionPoints":"First Mongol invasion → Iltutmish\nJalal-ud-din Khalji → Not first\nAla-ud-din Khalji → Mongol invasions\n1303 → Delhi siege\nMuhammad-bin-Tughlaq → No Mongol territorial loss"},
{"no":58,"text":"With reference to Indian history, who of the following were known as \"Kulah-Daran\"?","A":"Arab merchants","B":"Qalandars","C":"Persian calligraphists","D":"Sayyids","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Kulah-Daran","Sayyids","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Kulah-Daran literally refers to people who wore the kulah (conical cap). In medieval Indian history, the term was used for the Sayyids, who claimed descent from the Prophet Muhammad through his family. Sayyids often enjoyed a position of respect and influence in medieval Islamic society. Hence, the correct answer is D. Sayyids.","revisionPoints":"Kulah-Daran → Sayyids\nKulah → Conical cap\nSayyids → Prophet's descendants\nMedieval India → Persian influence\nSayyids → Social prestige"},
{"no":59,"text":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\n1. The Dutch established their factories/warehouses on the east coast on lands granted to them by Gajapati rulers.\n2. Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate.\n3. The English East India Company established a factory at Madras on a plot of land leased from a representative of the Vijayanagara Empire.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Dutch in India","Portuguese in India","English East India Company","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Dutch established factories on the east coast, but the lands were not granted by the Gajapati rulers as stated.\nStatement 2 is correct: In 1510, Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate, establishing Portuguese control.\nStatement 3 is correct: The English East India Company established its factory at Madras (Chennai) on land obtained from Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka, a local representative of the Vijayanagara rulers. Hence, the correct answer is B. 2 and 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Dutch → East coast factories\nAlbuquerque → Captured Goa\nGoa → 1510\nMadras → English factory\nVenkatadri Nayaka → Land grant"},
{"no":60,"text":"According to Kautilya's Arthashastra, which of the following are correct?\n1. A person could be a slave as a result of a judicial punishment.\n2. If a female slave bore her master a son, she was legally free.\n3. If a son born to a female slave was fathered by her master, the son was entitled to the legal status of the master's son.\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Arthashastra","Slavery","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"According to Kautilya’s Arthashastra, Statement 1 is incorrect because judicial punishment did not make a person a slave.\nStatement 2 is correct because a female slave who bore her master a son could gain freedom.\nStatement 3 is also correct because a son born to a female slave and fathered by her master was treated as the master’s legitimate son. Hence, Statements 2 and 3 are correct → Answer: B.","revisionPoints":"Arthashastra → Kautilya\nFemale slave → Freedom after son\nMaster’s son → Legal status\nJudicial punishment → Not slavery\nAnswer → 2 and 3 only"},
{"no":61,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Tight monetary policy of the US Federal Reserve could lead to capital flight.\n2. Capital flight may increase the interest cost of firms with existing External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs).\n3. Devaluation of domestic currency decreases the currency risk associated with ECBs.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["US Federal Reserve","External Commercial Borrowings","Capital Flight","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Tight monetary policy by the US Federal Reserve means higher interest rates, which can attract global capital toward the US and cause capital flight from emerging economies like India.\nStatement 2 is correct because capital outflow can weaken the domestic currency and increase the domestic-currency cost of servicing External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs).\nStatement 3 is incorrect because devaluation of the domestic currency increases, rather than decreases, the currency risk and repayment burden of foreign-currency ECBs. Hence, Statements 1 and 2 are correct → Answer: A.","revisionPoints":"US Fed tight policy → Capital outflow\nHigher US rates → Capital flight\nECBs → Foreign currency loans\nCurrency depreciation → Higher ECB burden\nDevaluation → Higher currency risk"},
{"no":62,"text":"Consider the following States:\n1. Andhra Pradesh\n2. Kerala\n3. Himachal Pradesh\n4. Tripura\nHow many of the above are generally known as tea-producing States?","A":"Only one State","B":"Only two States","C":"Only three States","D":"All four States","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Tea Producing States","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All four States are tea-producing States in India. Andhra Pradesh produces tea in the Eastern Ghats, while Kerala is known for tea plantations in Munnar. Himachal Pradesh produces tea mainly in Kangra, and Tripura is an important tea-producing State in Northeast India. Hence, all four States are correct → Answer: D.","revisionPoints":"Andhra Pradesh → Eastern Ghats\nKerala → Munnar\nHimachal Pradesh → Kangra\nTripura → Northeast India\nAll four → Tea-producing States"},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In India, credit rating agencies are regulated by Reserve Bank of India.\n2. The rating agency popularly known as ICRA is a public limited company.\n3. Brickwork Ratings is an Indian credit rating agency.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Credit Rating Agencies","ICRA","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect because credit rating agencies in India are regulated by SEBI (Securities and Exchange Board of India), not the RBI.\nStatement 2 is correct because ICRA, originally Investment Information and Credit Rating Agency of India Limited, is a public limited company.\nStatement 3 is also correct because Brickwork Ratings is an Indian credit rating agency. Hence, Statements 2 and 3 are correct → Answer: B.","revisionPoints":"Credit rating agencies → SEBI\nICRA → Public limited company\nICRA → Credit rating agency\nBrickwork Ratings → Indian CRA\nAnswer → 2 and 3 only"},
{"no":64,"text":"With reference to the Banks Board Bureau (BBB), which of the following statements are correct?\n1. The Governor of RBI is the Chairman of BBB.\n2. BBB recommends for the selection of heads of Public Sector Banks.\n3. BBB helps the Public Sector Banks in developing strategies and capital raising plans.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Banks Board Bureau","Public Sector Banks","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect because the RBI Governor is not the Chairman of the Banks Board Bureau (BBB).\nStatement 2 is correct because BBB recommends suitable candidates for the appointment of heads of Public Sector Banks and other financial institutions.\nStatement 3 is also correct because BBB assists Public Sector Banks in developing strategies and capital-raising plans. Hence, Statements 2 and 3 are correct → Answer: B.","revisionPoints":"BBB → Public Sector Banks\nRBI Governor → Not Chairman\nBBB → Recommends bank heads\nBBB → Strategy development\nBBB → Capital raising plans"},
{"no":65,"text":"With reference to Convertible Bonds, consider the following statements:\n1. As there is an option to exchange the bond for equity, Convertible Bonds pay a lower rate of interest.\n2. The option to convert to equity affords the bondholder a degree of indexation to rising consumer prices.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Convertible Bonds","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct because a convertible bond gives the bondholder an option to convert the bond into equity shares. Since this conversion option provides an additional benefit, convertible bonds generally offer a lower interest rate than similar non-convertible bonds.\nStatement 2 is also correct because if consumer prices rise, the value of the company’s equity may rise over time, giving the bondholder some protection against inflation through the conversion option. Hence, both Statements 1 and 2 are correct → Answer: C.","revisionPoints":"Convertible bond → Debt + equity option\nConversion option → Lower interest\nBondholder → Can get shares\nRising prices → Equity value may rise\nInflation protection → Degree of indexation\nAnswer → Both 1 and 2"},
{"no":66,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)\n2. Missile Technology Control Regime (MTCR)\n3. Shanghai Cooperation Organisation (SCO)\nIndia is a member of which of the above?\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["AIIB","MTCR","SCO","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"India is a member of all three organisations. India is a founding member of the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB), joined the Missile Technology Control Regime (MTCR) in 2018, and became a full member of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation (SCO) in 2017. Hence, all three are correct → Answer: D.","revisionPoints":"AIIB → India is founding member\nMTCR → India joined in 2018\nSCO → India joined in 2017\nAIIB → Infrastructure financing\nMTCR → Missile proliferation control\nSCO → Regional cooperation\nAnswer → 1, 2 and 3"},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Vietnam has been one of the fastest growing economies in the world in recent years.\n2. Vietnam is led by a multi-party political system.\n3. Vietnam's economic growth is linked to its integration with global supply chains and focus on exports.\n4. For a long time Vietnam's low labour costs and stable exchange rates have attracted global manufacturers.\n5. Vietnam has the most productive e-service sector in the Indo-Pacific region.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"2 and 4","B":"3 and 5","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1 and 2","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Vietnam","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Vietnam has been one of the fastest-growing economies in recent decades.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because Vietnam follows a single-party political system led by the Communist Party of Vietnam, not a multi-party system.\nStatement 3 is correct because Vietnam's growth is strongly linked to exports, foreign investment and integration into global supply chains.\nStatement 4 is correct because relatively low labour costs and economic stability have attracted global manufacturers.\nStatement 5 is incorrect because Vietnam is not established as having the most productive e-service sector in the Indo-Pacific. Hence, Statements 1, 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Vietnam → Fast-growing economy\nPolitical system → Single-party\nGrowth → Export-oriented\nGlobal supply chains → Integrated\nLabour costs → Relatively low\nFDI → Manufacturing hub\nAnswer → 1, 3 and 4"},
{"no":68,"text":"In India, which one of the following is responsible for maintaining price stability by controlling inflation?","A":"Department of Consumer Affairs","B":"Expenditure Management Commission","C":"Financial Stability and Development Council","D":"Reserve Bank of India","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["RBI","Price Stability","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is responsible for maintaining price stability while keeping economic growth in mind. RBI controls inflation mainly through monetary policy, such as changing the repo rate, managing liquidity and influencing credit conditions in the economy. The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) determines the policy repo rate to help keep inflation within the prescribed target range. Hence, Answer: D — Reserve Bank of India.","revisionPoints":"Price stability → RBI\nInflation control → Monetary policy\nRepo rate → Key tool\nMPC → Sets policy rate\nRBI → Inflation target\nAnswer → RBI (D)"},
{"no":69,"text":"With reference to Non-Fungible Tokens (NFTs), consider the following statements:\n1. They enable the digital representation of physical assets.\n2. They are unique cryptographic tokens that exist on a blockchain.\n3. They can be traded or exchanged at equivalency and therefore can be used as a medium of commercial transactions.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["NFTs","Blockchain","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: NFTs can represent ownership or rights connected to physical assets, such as artwork or collectibles, in digital form.\nStatement 2 is correct because NFTs are unique cryptographic tokens stored and tracked on a blockchain.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because NFTs are non-fungible, meaning each token is unique and cannot generally be exchanged on a one-to-one equivalent basis like currency or fungible tokens. Hence, Statements 1 and 2 are correct → Answer: A.","revisionPoints":"NFT → Non-Fungible Token\nNFT → Unique digital token\nBlockchain → NFT technology\nPhysical assets → Can be represented\nNon-fungible → Not interchangeable\nAnswer → 1 and 2 only"},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nReservoir\nState\n1. Ghataprabha\nTelangana\n2. Gandhi Sagar\nMadhya Pradesh\n3. Indira Sagar\nAndhra Pradesh\n4. Maithon\nChhattisgarh\nHow many pairs given above are not correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Reservoirs","Dams","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Ghataprabha Reservoir is in Karnataka, not Telangana.\nPair 2 is correct: Gandhi Sagar is in Madhya Pradesh.\nPair 3 is incorrect: Indira Sagar is in Madhya Pradesh, not Andhra Pradesh.\nPair 4 is incorrect: Maithon Reservoir is in Jharkhand, not Chhattisgarh. Therefore, 3 pairs are incorrectly matched → Answer: C.","revisionPoints":"Ghataprabha → Karnataka\nGandhi Sagar → Madhya Pradesh\nIndira Sagar → Madhya Pradesh\nMaithon → Jharkhand\nIncorrect pairs → 1, 3, 4"},
{"no":71,"text":"In India, which one of the following compiles information on industrial disputes, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs in factories employing workers?","A":"Central Statistics Office","B":"Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade","C":"Labour Bureau","D":"National Technical Manpower Information System","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Labour Bureau","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Labour Bureau under the Ministry of Labour and Employment collects and compiles information related to industrial disputes, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs. It provides important labour and employment statistics used for policy-making and analysis. Therefore, the correct answer is C. Labour Bureau.","revisionPoints":"Labour Bureau → Labour statistics\nIndustrial disputes → Labour Bureau\nClosures → Data collection\nRetrenchments → Data collection\nLay-offs → Data collection\nMinistry → Labour & Employment"},
{"no":72,"text":"In India, what is the role of the Coal Controller's Organisation (CCO)?\n1. CCO is the major source of Coal Statistics in Government of India.\n2. It monitors progress of development of Captive Coal/Lignite blocks.\n3. It hears any objection to the Government's notification relating to acquisition of coal-bearing areas.\n4. It ensures that coal mining companies deliver the coal to end users in the prescribed time.\nOptions:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Coal Controller’s Organisation","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Coal Controller’s Organisation (CCO) is a major source of official coal statistics for the Government of India.\nStatement 2 is correct because CCO monitors the development and progress of captive coal/lignite blocks.\nStatement 3 is correct because it has the authority to hear objections related to government notifications for the acquisition of coal-bearing areas.\nStatement 4 is incorrect because CCO does not ensure that coal companies deliver coal to end users within a prescribed time. Hence, Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct","revisionPoints":"CCO → Coal statistics\nCCO → Captive coal blocks\nCCO → Lignite blocks\nCCO → Acquisition objections\nCoal delivery → Not CCO's role"},
{"no":73,"text":"If a particular area is brought under the Fifth Schedule of the Constitution of India, which one of the following best reflects the consequence of it?","A":"This would prevent the transfer of land of tribal people to non-tribal people.","B":"This would create a local self-governing body in that area.","C":"This would convert that area into a Union Territory.","D":"The State having such areas would be declared a Special Category State.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fifth Schedule","Tribal Areas","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Fifth Schedule of the Indian Constitution deals with the administration and control of Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in states other than Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram. The Governor has special powers to regulate or restrict the transfer of tribal land to non-tribal persons, helping to protect tribal land and interests. Therefore, Answer: A.","revisionPoints":"Fifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas\nFocus → Tribal protection\nGovernor → Special powers\nTribal land → Protected\nLand transfer → Can be restricted"},
{"no":74,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The India Sanitation Coalition is a platform to promote sustainable sanitation and is funded by the Government of India and the World Health Organization.\n2. The National Institute of Urban Affairs is an apex body of the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs and provides innovative solutions to address the challenges of Urban India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["India Sanitation Coalition","National Institute of Urban Affairs","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The India Sanitation Coalition (ISC) is a platform that promotes sustainable sanitation, but it is not funded by the Government of India and WHO as stated.\nStatement 2 is also incorrect: The National Institute of Urban Affairs (NIUA) is an autonomous institute and not an apex body of the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs. It works on research, capacity building and policy support for urban development. Hence, neither Statement 1 nor Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"ISC → Sustainable sanitation\nISC → Multi-stakeholder platform\nNIUA → Autonomous institute\nNIUA → Urban research\nNIUA → Policy & capacity building"},
{"no":75,"text":"Which one of the following has been constituted under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986?","A":"Central Water Commission","B":"Central Ground Water Board","C":"Central Ground Water Authority","D":"National Water Development Agency","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Environment Protection Act 1986","Central Ground Water Authority","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Central Ground Water Authority (CGWA) was constituted under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 to regulate and control the development and management of groundwater resources. It can regulate groundwater extraction, especially in areas where groundwater is overexploited. The Central Water Commission, Central Ground Water Board and National Water Development Agency were not constituted under this Act.","revisionPoints":"CGWA → Environment Protection Act, 1986\nCGWA → Groundwater regulation\nGroundwater → Extraction control\nCWC → Water resources\nCGWB → Groundwater assessment"},
{"no":76,"text":"With reference to the \"United Nations Credentials Committee\", consider the following statements:\n1. It is a committee set up by the UN Security Council and works under its supervision.\n2. It traditionally meets in March, June and September every year.\n3. It assesses the credentials of all UN members before submitting a report to the General Assembly for approval.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"3 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1 and 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["United Nations Credentials Committee","UN General Assembly","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The UN Credentials Committee is established by the UN General Assembly, not the Security Council.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: It does not traditionally meet in March, June and September every year; its meetings are linked to the sessions of the General Assembly.\nStatement 3 is correct: The Committee examines the credentials of representatives of UN Member States and submits its report to the General Assembly for approval. Hence, only Statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Credentials Committee → UN General Assembly\nNot under → Security Council\nRole → Examines credentials\nReport → General Assembly\nMembers → UN representatives"},
{"no":77,"text":"Which one of the following statements best describes the \"Polar Code\"?","A":"It is the international code of safety for ships operating in polar waters.","B":"It is the agreement of the countries around the North Pole regarding the demarcation of their territories in the polar region.","C":"It is a set of norms to be followed by the countries whose scientists undertake research studies in the North Pole and South Pole.","D":"It is a trade and security agreement of the member countries of the Arctic Council.","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Law","tags":["Polar Code","IMO","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Polar Code is an international set of mandatory rules developed by the International Maritime Organization (IMO) for ships operating in Arctic and Antarctic waters. It covers issues such as ship safety, navigation, pollution prevention and protection of the polar environment. It does not deal with territorial boundaries or scientific research agreements.","revisionPoints":"Polar Code → IMO\nApplies to → Polar waters\nCovers → Arctic + Antarctic\nFocus → Ship safety\nAlso → Pollution prevention\nAim → Polar environment protection"},
{"no":78,"text":"With reference to the United Nations General Assembly, consider the following statements:\n1. The UN General Assembly can grant observer status to non-member States.\n2. Inter-governmental organisations can seek observer status in the UN General Assembly.\n3. Permanent Observers in the UN General Assembly can maintain missions at the UN headquarters.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UN General Assembly","Observer Status","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All three statements are correct. The UN General Assembly (UNGA) can grant observer status to non-member States, allowing them to participate in its work without being full members. Intergovernmental organisations can also receive observer status. Permanent Observers, such as the Holy See and the State of Palestine, can maintain permanent missions at UN Headquarters and participate in UN proceedings.","revisionPoints":"UNGA → Observer status\nNon-member States → Can be observers\nIGOs → Can seek observer status\nPermanent Observers → Maintain missions\nUN Headquarters → New York\nObserver → No full voting rights"},
{"no":79,"text":"With reference to the \"Tea Board\" in India, consider the following statements:\n1. The Tea Board is a statutory body.\n2. It is a regulatory body attached to the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\n3. The Tea Board's Head Office is situated in Bengaluru.\n4. The Board has overseas offices at Dubai and Moscow.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 4","C":"3 and 4","D":"1 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Tea Board","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: The Tea Board of India is a statutory body established under the Tea Act, 1953.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because it functions under the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, not the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because its headquarters is in Kolkata, not Bengaluru.\nStatement 4 is correct because the Tea Board has overseas offices in Dubai and Moscow.","revisionPoints":"Tea Board → Statutory body\nAct → Tea Act, 1953\nMinistry → Commerce & Industry\nHeadquarters → Kolkata\nOverseas offices → Dubai + Moscow"},
{"no":80,"text":"Which one of the following best describes the term \"greenwashing\"?","A":"Conveying a false impression that a company's products are eco-friendly and environmentally sound.","B":"Non-inclusion of ecological/environmental costs in the Annual Financial Statements of a country.","C":"Ignoring the disastrous ecological consequences while undertaking infrastructure development.","D":"Making mandatory provisions for environmental costs in a government project/programme.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Greenwashing","ESG","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Greenwashing refers to the practice of giving a false or misleading impression that a company, product or service is environmentally friendly. Companies may use attractive environmental claims or labels to appear more sustainable than they actually are. Therefore, Answer: A — Conveying a false impression that a company's products are eco-friendly and environmentally sound.","revisionPoints":"Greenwashing → Misleading green claims\nAim → Appear eco-friendly\nUsed by → Companies/brands\nProblem → False sustainability\nExample → Unverified eco-labels"},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. High clouds primarily reflect solar radiation and cool the surface of the Earth.\n2. Low clouds have a high absorption of infrared radiation emanating from the Earth's surface and thus cause warming effect.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Clouds","Radiation Balance","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: High clouds, such as cirrus clouds, are generally thin and high-altitude clouds. They allow much of the incoming solar radiation to pass through but can trap outgoing infrared radiation, producing a warming effect.\nStatement 2 is also incorrect: Low clouds generally reflect a large amount of incoming solar radiation back to space, producing a cooling effect.","revisionPoints":"High clouds → Warming effect\nHigh clouds → Trap infrared radiation\nLow clouds → Cooling effect\nLow clouds → Reflect sunlight\nSolar radiation → Shortwave\nEarth radiation → Infrared"},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Bidibidi is a large refugee settlement in north-western Kenya.\n2. Some people who fled from South Sudan civil war live in Bidibidi.\n3. Some people who fled from civil war in Somalia live in Dadaab refugee complex in Kenya.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["South Sudan","Refugee Settlements","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Bidibidi is a large refugee settlement in north-western Uganda, not Kenya.\nStatement 2 is correct because many refugees from South Sudan live in Bidibidi after fleeing the civil war.\nStatement 3 is correct because the Dadaab refugee complex in Kenya hosts refugees, including people who fled conflict and civil war in Somalia.","revisionPoints":"Bidibidi → Uganda\nBidibidi → South Sudan refugees\nDadaab → Kenya\nDadaab → Somali refugees\nSouth Sudan → Civil war\nSomalia → Conflict"},
{"no":83,"text":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Armenia\n2. Azerbaijan\n3. Croatia\n4. Romania\n5. Uzbekistan\nWhich of the above are members of the Organization of Turkic States?","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 5","D":"3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Organization of Turkic States","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Organization of Turkic States (OTS) is a regional organisation that promotes cooperation among Turkic-speaking countries. Azerbaijan and Uzbekistan are members of the OTS. Armenia and Romania are not members, while Croatia is also not a member.","revisionPoints":"OTS → Turkic cooperation\nAzerbaijan → Member\nUzbekistan → Member\nArmenia → Not member\nRomania → Not member\nCroatia → Not member"},
{"no":84,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Gujarat has the largest solar park in India.\n2. Kerala has a fully solar powered International Airport.\n3. Goa has the largest floating solar photovoltaic project in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Solar Parks","Solar Airport","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Bhadla Solar Park in Rajasthan is the largest solar park in India, not Gujarat.\nStatement 2 is correct: Cochin International Airport (Kerala) became the world's first fully solar-powered airport in terms of its electricity needs through its large solar installations.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Goa does not have the largest floating solar photovoltaic project in India","revisionPoints":"Bhadla Solar Park → Rajasthan\nBhadla → Largest solar park\nCochin Airport → Kerala\nCochin Airport → Solar-powered\nFloating solar → Not Goa"},
{"no":85,"text":"With reference to the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), consider the following statements:\n1. A coastal State has the right to establish the breadth of its territorial sea up to a limit not exceeding 12 nautical miles, measured from baseline determined in accordance with the Convention.\n2. Ships of all States, whether coastal or land-locked, enjoy the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea.\n3. The Exclusive Economic Zone shall not extend beyond 200 nautical miles from the baseline from which the breadth of the territorial sea is measured.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Law","tags":["UNCLOS","Territorial Sea","Exclusive Economic Zone","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"All three statements are correct. Under UNCLOS, a coastal State can establish its territorial sea up to 12 nautical miles from its baseline. Ships of all States, including coastal and land-locked States, have the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea. The Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) can extend up to, but not beyond, 200 nautical miles from the baseline.","revisionPoints":"Territorial sea → 12 nautical miles\nInnocent passage → All States\nLandlocked States → Right of passage\nEEZ → Up to 200 nautical miles\nUNCLOS → Law of the Sea\nBaseline → Measurement point"},
{"no":86,"text":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the issue with Senkaku Islands, sometimes mentioned in the news?","A":"It is generally believed that they are artificial islands made by a country around South China Sea.","B":"China and Japan engage in maritime disputes over these islands in East China Sea.","C":"A permanent American military base has been set up there to help Taiwan increase its defence capabilities.","D":"Though International Court of Justice declared them as no man's land, some South-East Asian countries claim them.","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Senkaku Islands","East China Sea","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"The Senkaku Islands are a group of uninhabited islands in the East China Sea. They are administered by Japan, but China also claims sovereignty over them (China refers to them as the Diaoyu Islands). This has led to continuing maritime and territorial tensions between China and Japan.","revisionPoints":"Senkaku Islands → East China Sea\nAdministered by → Japan\nClaimed by → China\nChina’s name → Diaoyu Islands\nIssue → Territorial dispute\nRegion → East Asia"},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nCountry\nImportant reason for being in the news recently\n1. Chad\nSetting up of permanent military base by China\n2. Guinea\nSuspension of Constitution and Government by military\n3. Lebanon\nSevere and prolonged economic depression\n4. Tunisia\nSuspension of Parliament by President\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Chad","Guinea","Lebanon","Tunisia","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Chad was not the country where China set up a permanent military base; China’s overseas military base is in Djibouti.\nPair 2 is correct because Guinea's military suspended the Constitution and dissolved the government after the 2021 coup.\nPair 3 is correct because Lebanon faced a severe and prolonged economic crisis.\nPair 4 is correct because Tunisia's President suspended Parliament in 2021.","revisionPoints":"Chad → Not Chinese base\nDjibouti → Chinese military base\nGuinea → Military coup\nLebanon → Economic crisis\nTunisia → Parliament suspended\nCorrect pairs → 2, 3 and 4"},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nRegion often mentioned in news\nCountry\n1. Anatolia\nTurkey\n2. Amhara\nEthiopia\n3. Cabo Delgado\nSpain\n4. Catalonia\nItaly\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Anatolia","Amhara","Cabo Delgado","Catalonia","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is correct: Anatolia is the large peninsula that forms most of Turkey.\nPair 2 is correct: Amhara is a major regional state in Ethiopia.\nPair 3 is incorrect: Cabo Delgado is a province in Mozambique, not Spain.\nPair 4 is incorrect: Catalonia is an autonomous community in Spain, not Italy.","revisionPoints":"Anatolia → Turkey\nAmhara → Ethiopia\nCabo Delgado → Mozambique\nCatalonia → Spain\nCorrect pairs → 1 and 2"},
{"no":89,"text":"With reference to Indian laws about wildlife protection, consider the following statements:\n1. Wild animals are the sole property of the government.\n2. When a wild animal is declared protected, such animal is entitled for equal protection whether it is found in protected areas or outside.\n3. Apprehension of a protected wild animal becoming a danger to human life is sufficient ground for its capture or killing.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Wildlife Protection Act","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Wild animals are not simply the sole property of the government; the law regulates their protection, possession, hunting and trade.\nStatement 2 is correct: Once a wild animal is protected under the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, the protection applies whether it is found inside or outside a protected area.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Mere apprehension that an animal may become dangerous to human life is not by itself sufficient; the Act prescribes specific conditions and procedures for capture or killing.","revisionPoints":"Wildlife Protection Act → 1972\nProtected animals → Protection everywhere\nProtected areas → Not the only protection\nCapture/kill → Specific legal conditions\nMere apprehension → Not sufficient"},
{"no":90,"text":"Certain species of which one of the following organisms are well known as cultivators of fungi?","A":"Ant","B":"Cockroach","C":"Crab","D":"Spider","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Fungus-farming Ants","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Ants, especially leaf-cutter ants, are well known for cultivating fungi. They collect pieces of leaves and use them as a substrate to grow fungus inside their nests. The ants then feed on this cultivated fungus, making it an example of symbiotic mutualism between ants and fungi.","revisionPoints":"Leaf-cutter ants → Fungus cultivation\nAnts → Grow fungi\nLeaves → Fungus substrate\nFungus → Food for ants\nRelationship → Mutualism"},
{"no":91,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nSite of Ashoka's major rock edicts\nLocation in the State\n1. Dhauli\nOdisha\n2. Erragudi\nAndhra Pradesh\n3. Jaugada\nMadhya Pradesh\n4. Kalsi\nKarnataka\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ashoka","Rock Edicts","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is correct: Dhauli is in Odisha. Pair 2 is correct: Erragudi (Yerragudi) is in Andhra Pradesh. Pair 3 is incorrect: Jaugada is in Odisha, not Madhya Pradesh. Pair 4 is incorrect: Kalsi is in Uttarakhand, not Karnataka. Therefore, only two pairs are correctly matched","revisionPoints":"Dhauli → Odisha\nErragudi → Andhra Pradesh\nJaugada → Odisha\nKalsi → Uttarakhand\nCorrect pairs → 1 and 2"},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nKing\nDynasty\n1. Nannuka\nChandela\n2. Jayashakti\nParamara\n3. Nagabhata II\nGurjara-Pratihara\n4. Bhoja\nRashtrakuta\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"Only three pairs","D":"All four pairs","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Chandela","Paramara","Gurjara-Pratihara","Rashtrakuta","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Pair 1 is correct: Nannuka was an early ruler of the Chandela dynasty. Pair 2 is incorrect: Jayashakti (Jeja) belonged to the Chandela dynasty, not the Paramara dynasty. Pair 3 is correct: Nagabhata II was a prominent ruler of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty. Pair 4 is incorrect: Bhoja, the famous ruler associated with this context, belonged to the Paramara dynasty, not the Rashtrakuta dynasty. Therefore, only two pairs are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Nannuka → Chandela\nJayashakti → Chandela\nNagabhata II → Gurjara-Pratihara\nBhoja → Paramara\nCorrect pairs → 1 and 3"},
{"no":93,"text":"Which one of the following statements about Sangam literature in ancient South India is correct?","A":"Sangam poems are devoid of any reference to material culture.","B":"The social classification of Varna was known to Sangam poets.","C":"Sangam poems have no reference to warrior ethic.","D":"Sangam literature refers to magical forces as irrational.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Sangam Literature","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement B is correct: Sangam literature shows that the varna-based social classification was known to the Sangam poets. The poems also contain references to material culture, warfare, kingship, trade and social life. Therefore, Statements A and C are incorrect because Sangam literature does discuss material culture and warrior ethics. Statement D is incorrect because Sangam literature contains references to magical and supernatural beliefs, rather than simply dismissing them as irrational.","revisionPoints":"Sangam literature → Ancient Tamil\nVarna system → Known\nMaterial culture → Mentioned\nWarrior ethic → Present\nSupernatural beliefs → Mentioned\nSociety → Kingship + trade"},
{"no":94,"text":"\"Yogavāsiṣṭha\" was translated into Persian by Nizamuddin Panipati during the reign of:","A":"Akbar","B":"Humayun","C":"Shahjahan","D":"Aurangzeb","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Yogavāsiṣṭha","Mughal Translations","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Yogavāsiṣṭha is an important Sanskrit philosophical text dealing with Vedanta, spirituality and the nature of reality. During the reign of Akbar, it was translated from Sanskrit into Persian by Nizamuddin Panipati. Akbar encouraged the translation of several Sanskrit works into Persian as part of his interest in Indian religions and philosophy.","revisionPoints":"Yogavāsiṣṭha → Sanskrit text\nSubject → Philosophy & spirituality\nPersian translator → Nizamuddin Panipati\nTranslation period → Akbar’s reign\nAkbar → Sanskrit–Persian translations\nAnswer → A. Akbar"},
{"no":95,"text":"The world's second tallest statue in sitting pose of Ramanuja was inaugurated by the Prime Minister of India at Hyderabad recently. Which one of the following statements correctly represents the teachings of Ramanuja?","A":"The best means of salvation was devotion.","B":"Vedas are eternal, self-existent and wholly authoritative.","C":"Logical arguments were essential means for the highest bliss.","D":"Salvation was to be obtained through meditation.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Ramanuja","Vishishtadvaita","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Ramanuja was a major philosopher of the Bhakti tradition and the chief exponent of Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism). He taught that devotion (Bhakti) to God, especially Vishnu, was the best means of attaining salvation. He emphasized a personal relationship with God rather than salvation through mere intellectual knowledge or meditation.","revisionPoints":"Ramanuja → Vishishtadvaita\nPhilosophy → Qualified non-dualism\nGod → Personal God (Vishnu)\nSalvation → Bhakti\nPath → Devotion\nTradition → Bhakti movement\nAnswer → A. Devotion"},
{"no":96,"text":"The Prime Minister recently inaugurated the new Circuit House near Somnath Temple at Veraval. Which of the following statements are correct regarding Somnath Temple?\n1. Somnath Temple is one of the Jyotirlinga shrines.\n2. A description of Somnath Temple was given by Al-Biruni.\n3. Pran Pratishtha of Somnath Temple (installation of the present day temple) was done by President S. Radhakrishnan.\nOptions:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temples & Shrines","tags":["Somnath Temple","Jyotirlinga","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Somnath Temple, located at Prabhas Patan near Veraval in Gujarat, is one of the 12 Jyotirlinga shrines of Lord Shiva, so Statement 1 is correct. The Persian scholar Al-Biruni mentioned Somnath in his writings, making Statement 2 correct. Statement 3 is incorrect because the Pran Pratishtha of the present temple was performed in 1951 by President Dr. Rajendra Prasad, not S. Radhakrishnan.","revisionPoints":"Somnath → Gujarat\nLocation → Prabhas Patan\nDeity → Lord Shiva\nJyotirlingas → One of 12\nAl-Biruni → Described Somnath\nPresent temple → 1951\nPran Pratishtha → Rajendra Prasad"},
{"no":97,"text":"Which one of the following statements best describes the role of B cells and T cells in the human body?","A":"They protect the body from environmental allergens.","B":"They alleviate the body's pain and inflammation.","C":"They act as immunosuppressants in the body.","D":"They protect the body from the diseases caused by pathogens.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["B Cells","T Cells","Immunology","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"B cells and T cells are important types of lymphocytes in the immune system. B cells mainly produce antibodies that help fight pathogens, while T cells help destroy infected cells and coordinate immune responses. Together, they provide protection against diseases caused by pathogens such as bacteria and viruses. Hence, the correct answer is D.","revisionPoints":"B cells → Antibody production\nT cells → Cell-mediated immunity\nB cells → Humoral immunity\nT cells → Kill infected cells\nBoth → Adaptive immunity\nMain role → Fight pathogens\nAnswer → D. Pathogen protection"},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Other than those made by humans, nanoparticles do not exist in nature.\n2. Nanoparticles of some metallic oxides are used in the manufacture of some cosmetics.\n3. Nanoparticles of some commercial products which enter the environment are unsafe for humans.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Nanoparticles","Nanotechnology","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect because nanoparticles can occur naturally, such as particles produced by volcanic eruptions, forest fires and weathering. Statement 2 is correct because nanoparticles of metal oxides such as titanium dioxide and zinc oxide are used in some cosmetics and sunscreens. Statement 3 is correct because some engineered nanoparticles released into the environment may pose health and environmental risks.","revisionPoints":"Nanoparticles → Natural + artificial\nNatural sources → Volcanoes, fires\nTiO₂ → Cosmetics\nZnO → Sunscreens\nEnvironmental release → Possible risks"},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nDNA Barcoding can be a tool to:\n1. Assess the age of a plant or animal.\n2. Distinguish among species that look alike.\n3. Identify undesirable animal or plant materials in processed foods.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["DNA Barcoding","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"DNA barcoding is a technique used to identify species by analysing a short, standard DNA sequence. It cannot normally determine the age of a plant or animal, so Statement 1 is incorrect. It can distinguish between closely related or visually similar species and can also identify unwanted or adulterated plant/animal materials in processed food.","revisionPoints":"DNA barcoding → Species identification\nUses → Short DNA sequence\nSimilar species → Can distinguish\nProcessed food → Detects adulteration\nAge determination → Not its purpose"},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Carbon monoxide\n2. Nitrogen oxide\n3. Ozone\n4. Sulphur dioxide\nExcess of which of the above in the environment is/are cause(s) of acid rain?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"4 only","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Acid Rain","UPSC","2022"],"expl":"Acid rain mainly occurs when sulphur dioxide (SO₂) and nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) are released into the atmosphere. They react with water vapour and form sulphuric acid and nitric acid, which fall to the ground with rain. Carbon monoxide and ozone are not the primary causes of acid rain.","revisionPoints":"Acid rain → SO₂ + NOₓ\nSO₂ → Sulphuric acid\nNOₓ → Nitric acid\nMajor sources → Industries, vehicles\nCO → Not acid rain\nOzone → Not primary cause"},
];
